WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.189
Film Courage: Should a screenwriter start
designing the movie poster before the script

00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:04.189
is written?

00:00:04.189 --> 00:00:05.330
Scott Kirkpatrick: No, I don’t think so.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:09.570
I mean I mentioned that in the Writing for
the Greenlight [Scott’s book] because that’s

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:10.570
how we did it.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:18.119
We already had the established connection
and track record of being able to pre-sell

00:00:18.119 --> 00:00:22.380
movies and to produce them and deliver them
and have them actually meet the expectations

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.900
of what we promise to deliver and we had a
whole marketing department that knew how to

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.260
create poster work specifically for the movie
buying audience.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:38.860
You can usually tell when an indie producer
has drafted their own artwork versus when

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:43.680
it’s done by a company that’s is used
to selling into the movie space because it

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.730
just has a completely different look.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:50.730
It’s not a marketable, punchy, pushy-type
poster.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.580
Not that there is anything wrong with it.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:58.010
It’s just that it’s not the type of stuff
that attracts the buyers from all over the

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.940
world or things of that nature.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.730
They get redesigned and redesigned throughout
the whole process.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:08.520
I think the screenwriter shouldn’t worry
about the stuff they’re not going to be

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:14.510
in a position (especially starting out) where
they have to presell their project to buyers.

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:18.410
They’re selling themselves…they are not
even selling their script really…they are

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:20.650
selling their talent to write.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:26.480
So they shouldn’t have to focus on the marketing
components of creating artwork, they should

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:34.960
focus on making sure that they’re producing
a library of work, a portfolio that they can

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:42.050
showcase that’s ideally all of the same
type of genre so they can just be the go-to

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:47.090
screenwriter who can deliver that type of
film quickly and reliably.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:51.410
Just as I am saying, we would sell movies
(pre-sell) movies off of the poster, people

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:55.130
would pay us and pre-buy them because they
knew we could deliver them.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.750
There’s a lot of companies that try to pre-sell
that fail.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.950
I’ve worked with ones that were very successful
because we’ve had the track record of being

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:04.700
able to reliably deliver on time good quality
products.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:08.690
That’s what the screenwriter wants to do
just from a writing perspective.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:13.670
Here’s my showcase of what I can do, this
proves a track record.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:19.600
If you give me the opportunity you can guarantee
and trust that I am going to get it done correctly

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:20.600
the first time.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.940
Film Courage: So why do you think those other
titles failed?

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.700
What was the reason?

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.290
I mean you said that there were others that
tried the same approach.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:34.560
Scott Kirkpatrick: Look pre-sales is this
really cool, fun idea of like “You know,

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.860
my idea is going to be so great, I’m just
going to get 20 countries to jump on board

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.480
and buy it.”

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.760
You really have to know how the pre-sale process
works, who the buyers are at these companies

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.830
that are willing to invest that money.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:56.370
You have to already produced a decent amount
of content that’s going to kind of prove

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:59.370
the fact that you know what you’re doing
and you can deliver movies on time that are

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:05.090
under budget or on budget that meet all the
technical specifications of that specific

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:06.349
country.

00:03:06.349 --> 00:03:11.819
It’s a delicate process and it’s not easy
to do and not all companies do it.

00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:14.870
There’s a lot of companies that have no
interest in even trying it.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:19.880
But the idea of selling a movie off of a poster
really only today happens at the pre-sale

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.570
stage and not every company does pre-sales.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.840
Big studios, they will come out and basically
say this is our slate.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.870
Independent companies, we have to go build
those relationships because we don’t have

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:32.530
output deals with everybody.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:38.750
We don’t have guaranteed buys in the majority
of countries the way studios do.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:44.620
So there are companies that try to do that
and have to be very…they have to have a

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:48.780
good reputation for being able to already
accomplish what they are selling themselves

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:49.650
to be able to do.

